{"applyIt":"Think about one thing that has been steady in your life, such as someone who keeps showing up for you or a kindness that has repeated over time. You might pause and name it as a reason to give thanks. You could say a short line of thanks aloud, the way the verse invites a group to do. If goodness is hard to see today, it is fine to start small, with one honest sentence of gratitude.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAHOT (Translators Amalgamated Hebrew OT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d","crossReferences":[{"ref":"PSA.106.1","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2CH.7.3","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"EZR.3.11","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"PSA.107.1","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"PSA.136.1-PSA.136.26","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2CH.5.13","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"PSA.118.1","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"JER.33.11","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"The word hodu, translated give thanks, is a command in a plural form, so it calls out to a group of people together rather than to one person alone. It is spoken here as a summons to a whole gathering.","historicalContext":"The verse sits in a passage of praise. The surrounding verses call on the heavens, the sea, the fields and the trees to rejoice before the LORD, and they say that he reigns and comes to judge the earth. A later line asks to be saved from the nations and gathered together, so this praise is linked to a people who need deliverance. The passage also mentions the ark of the LORD's covenant, before which Asaph and his brothers were left to minister continually. Chronicles was probably written after the exile, though its date and author are debated.","id":"1CH.16.34","lifeInBibleTimes":"Worship in ancient Israel was often sung and spoken aloud by a gathered community, and this passage fits that picture. Its lines about trees singing for joy and about saying the praise among the nations both point to praise that is voiced, not kept silent. The ark of the covenant, mentioned here, was the central sacred object of Israel's worship. It was a chest that was carried with poles and kept in a holy place, and the men who served before it were the ones trusted with its daily care.","meaning":"This short verse is a call to give thanks to the LORD, followed by the reason: he is good, and his loving kindness endures forever. The word the World English Bible renders as loving kindness is the Hebrew word for faithful, committed love that keeps a promise, the kind of love that holds within a covenant relationship. The verse does not say that thanks earns God's goodness. It names what is already true of him and invites people to answer with gratitude. Its wording is brief, which makes it easy to remember and to say back together.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"hebrew","textBasis":"leningrad","words":[{"gloss":"give thanks","lemma":"יָדָה","morphology":"HVhv2mp","strongs":"H3034","surface":"הוֹד֤וּ","transliteration":"ho.Du"},{"gloss":"to Yahweh","lemma":"יהוה","morphology":"HR/Npt","strongs":"H3068G","surface":"לַיהוָה֙","transliteration":"la.Yah.weh"},{"gloss":"for","lemma":"כִּי","morphology":"HTc","strongs":"H3588A","surface":"כִּ֣י","transliteration":"ki"},{"gloss":"[he is] good","lemma":"טוֹב","morphology":"HAamsa","strongs":"H2896A","surface":"ט֔וֹב","transliteration":"Tov"},{"gloss":"for","lemma":"כִּי","morphology":"HTc","strongs":"H3588A","surface":"כִּ֥י","transliteration":"ki"},{"gloss":"[is] for ever","lemma":"עוֹלָם","morphology":"HR/Ncmsa","strongs":"H5769G","surface":"לְעוֹלָ֖ם","transliteration":"le.'o.Lam"},{"gloss":"covenant loyalty his","lemma":"חֶ֫סֶד","morphology":"HNcmsc/Sp3ms","strongs":"H2617A","surface":"חַסְדּֽוֹ׃","transliteration":"chas.Do"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"The verse shows that God's character is the reason for thanks. He is good, and his covenant love, the steady loyalty he keeps to his people, does not run out. Thanks, in this picture, is a response to who God already is rather than a payment for something he has done. Psalm 136 repeats a line about his enduring love again and again through its many verses. Psalm 106:1, Psalm 107:1 and Psalm 118:1 open with a similar call to give thanks, showing that this kind of praise was part of Israel's songs across the Bible."}{"applyIt":"Think of one part of the good news you find hard to believe or explain. Read this verse slowly and notice that Paul does not ask you to be clever about it. You might simply speak to God about what feels foolish to you, and let the message of the cross be what it is: God's power at work. There is no need to win an argument before you rest in it.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAGNT (Translators Amalgamated Greek NT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d","crossReferences":[{"ref":"1CO.2.14","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ROM.1.16","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2TH.2.10","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.1.23-1CO.1.25","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2CO.4.3","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.1.21","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2CO.2.15-2CO.2.16","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.3.19","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"The Greek words men (indeed) and de (however) work as a pair in this verse. They set the two groups side by side, those perishing on one side and those being saved on the other, so the contrast is built into the sentence itself.","historicalContext":"Paul is writing to a church in Corinth that had split into groups and was arguing over who they followed. Just before this verse he says he was sent to preach the good news without clever wording, so the cross would not be emptied of its meaning. Corinth was a busy port city that was divided and troubled, and Paul's point about the cross speaks straight into that. The passage contrasts human cleverness with the cross, and this verse gives the reason the plain message matters: it looks like weakness to some and God's power to others.","id":"1CO.1.18","lifeInBibleTimes":"Crucifixion was a Roman method of execution, mostly used for criminals and rebels, and it was a public and deeply shameful death. The Greek word here is staurou, which refers to this kind of cross, so Paul's message named something most listeners would find horrifying rather than hopeful. Corinth was a busy port city where ships, traders and travelers from many places passed through, so many kinds of ideas and teachers were heard there. A crowded city of talkers and debaters helps explain why Paul speaks about wisdom so directly.","meaning":"Paul sums up his message in one contrast. The message about the cross, the death of Jesus on a cross, sounds like foolishness to those who are dying, which is the word the World English Bible uses for people headed toward being lost. But to us who are being saved, the same message is the power of God. The verse does not say the message changes. It says people respond to it in two different ways. Just before this, Paul said Christ sent him to preach the good news plainly, so that the cross would not lose its meaning. This verse explains why some people turn away from that message and others receive it as God's power.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"greek","textBasis":"critical","words":[{"gloss":"The","lemma":"ὁ","morphology":"T-NSM","strongs":"G3588","surface":"Ὁ","transliteration":"Ho"},{"gloss":"message","lemma":"λόγος","morphology":"N-NSM","strongs":"G3056","surface":"λόγος","transliteration":"logos"},{"gloss":"for","lemma":"γάρ","morphology":"CONJ","strongs":"G1063","surface":"γὰρ","transliteration":"gar"},{"gloss":"<the>","lemma":"ὁ","morphology":"T-NSM","strongs":"G3588","surface":"ὁ","transliteration":"ho"},{"gloss":"of the","lemma":"ὁ","morphology":"T-GSM","strongs":"G3588","surface":"τοῦ","transliteration":"tou"},{"gloss":"cross","lemma":"σταυρός","morphology":"N-GSM","strongs":"G4716","surface":"σταυροῦ","transliteration":"staurou"},{"gloss":"to those","lemma":"ὁ","morphology":"T-DPM","strongs":"G3588","surface":"τοῖς","transliteration":"tois"},{"gloss":"indeed","lemma":"μέν","morphology":"PRT","strongs":"G3303","surface":"μὲν","transliteration":"men"},{"gloss":"perishing","lemma":"ἀπολλύω","morphology":"V-PEP-DPM","strongs":"G0622","surface":"ἀπολλυμένοις","transliteration":"apollumenois"},{"gloss":"foolishness","lemma":"μωρία","morphology":"N-NSF","strongs":"G3472","surface":"μωρία","transliteration":"mōria"},{"gloss":"is,","lemma":"εἰμί","morphology":"V-PAI-3S","strongs":"G1510","surface":"ἐστίν,","transliteration":"estin"},{"gloss":"to those","lemma":"ὁ","morphology":"T-DPM","strongs":"G3588","surface":"τοῖς","transliteration":"tois"},{"gloss":"however","lemma":"δέ","morphology":"CONJ","strongs":"G1161","surface":"δὲ","transliteration":"de"},{"gloss":"being saved","lemma":"σῴζω","morphology":"V-PPP-DPM","strongs":"G4982","surface":"σῳζομένοις","transliteration":"sōzomenois"},{"gloss":"to us","lemma":"ἐγώ","morphology":"P-1DP","strongs":"G3165","surface":"ἡμῖν","transliteration":"hēmin"},{"gloss":"power","lemma":"δύναμις","morphology":"N-NSF","strongs":"G1411","surface":"δύναμις","transliteration":"dunamis"},{"gloss":"of God","lemma":"θεός","morphology":"N-GSM-T","strongs":"G2316","surface":"θεοῦ","transliteration":"theou"},{"gloss":"it is.","lemma":"εἰμί","morphology":"V-PAI-3S","strongs":"G1510","surface":"ἐστιν.","transliteration":"estin"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"The verse shows that God's power and human wisdom can look like opposites. The cross, a sign of weakness and defeat, is described here as God's power for those being saved. Salvation is presented as something God does, not something people earn by impressing others with clever arguments. Romans 1:16 uses similar language, calling the good news God's power for salvation to everyone who believes. The wider passage also notes that the world's wisdom did not lead people to know God, which points to the Bible's theme that God's ways are higher than human pride."}{"applyIt":"Think of a time when you felt pressure to go along with what others expected. You might pause, name the God you trust, and ask for the courage to speak honestly without knowing how things will turn out. Trust doesn't require a guaranteed result. You can bring the open question to God in prayer and let him hold it with you.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAHOT (Translators Amalgamated Hebrew OT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d","crossReferences":[{"ref":"ROM.8.31","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"LUK.1.37","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"HEB.7.25","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"PSA.27.1-PSA.27.2","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ISA.26.3-ISA.26.4","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"JER.1.8","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"JOB.5.19","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"DAN.6.20-DAN.6.22","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"Look at the repeated word for deliver: the verb yesheiziv appears in the verse as 'he will deliver', and the same idea comes back earlier as 'to deliver us'. The verse thus repeats the idea of rescue in both halves of the sentence.","historicalContext":"Nebuchadnezzar has just set the choice before the three men: worship the golden image or be thrown into a burning furnace at once. He has also asked who could rescue them from his hands. The three men reply by pointing to God. The book of Daniel tells of faithful Jews serving in foreign royal courts. Here the men do not argue about the furnace itself. They answer the threat by saying what they trust, and what they will do, whatever happens.","id":"DAN.3.17","lifeInBibleTimes":"In the king's order, a set of musical instruments acts as a signal. When the horn, flute, zither, lyre, harp, pipe, and other music sound, everyone is to fall down before the image. Ancient kings often used large public ceremonies to show loyalty, and music probably helped mark when the crowd should bow. Here the sound becomes a test of loyalty. The men's reply sets up the refusal they make in the surrounding text.","meaning":"The three men tell the king that their God can rescue them from the blazing furnace and can rescue them from the king's hand as well. The sentence opens with 'if it happens,' which leaves the outcome open. They do not say God must rescue them or promise exactly how it will go. Their claim is about God's power: he is able to deliver. The line also suggests their trust is in God, the one they serve, rather than in the king's threat. The reply continues in the surrounding text, where they say they still will not serve the king's gods, even if God does not rescue them.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"aramaic","textBasis":"leningrad","words":[{"gloss":"if","lemma":"הֵן","morphology":"AC","strongs":"H2006A","surface":"הֵ֣ן","transliteration":"hen"},{"gloss":"there","lemma":"אִיתַי","morphology":"ANcbsa","strongs":"H0383","surface":"אִיתַ֗י","transliteration":"'i.Tai"},{"gloss":"[is] God our","lemma":"אֱלָהּ","morphology":"ANcbsc/Sp1bp","strongs":"H0426","surface":"אֱלָהַ֙נָא֙","transliteration":"'e.la.Ha.na'"},{"gloss":"whom","lemma":"דִּי","morphology":"AC","strongs":"H1768","surface":"דִּֽי־","transliteration":"di-"},{"gloss":"we","lemma":"אֲנַ֫חְנָא","morphology":"APp1bp","strongs":"H0586","surface":"אֲנַ֣חְנָא","transliteration":"'a.Nach.na'"},{"gloss":"[are] paying reverence","lemma":"פְּלַח","morphology":"AVqrmpa","strongs":"H6399","surface":"פָֽלְחִ֔ין","transliteration":"fa.le.Chin"},{"gloss":"[is] being able","lemma":"יְכִל","morphology":"AVqrmsa","strongs":"H3202","surface":"יָכִ֖ל","transliteration":"ya.Khil"},{"gloss":"to deliver us","lemma":"שֵׁיזִב","morphology":"AR/Vecc/Sp1bp","strongs":"H7804","surface":"לְשֵׁיזָבוּתַ֑נָא","transliteration":"le.shei.za.vu.Ta.na'"},{"gloss":"from","lemma":"מִן־","morphology":"AR","strongs":"H4481","surface":"מִן־","transliteration":"min-"},{"gloss":"[the] furnace of","lemma":"אַתּוּן","morphology":"ANcbsc","strongs":"H0861","surface":"אַתּ֨וּן","transliteration":"'a.Tun"},{"gloss":"fire the","lemma":"נוּר","morphology":"ANcbsd/Ta","strongs":"H5135","surface":"נוּרָ֧א","transliteration":"nu.Ra'"},{"gloss":"<the> burning","lemma":"יְקַד","morphology":"AVqrfsd/Ta","strongs":"H3345","surface":"יָקִֽדְתָּ֛א","transliteration":"ya.kid.Ta'"},{"gloss":"and from","lemma":"מִן־","morphology":"AC/R","strongs":"H4481","surface":"וּמִן־","transliteration":"u.min-"},{"gloss":"hand your","lemma":"יַד","morphology":"ANcbsc/Sp2ms","strongs":"H3028","surface":"יְדָ֥ךְ","transliteration":"ye.Dakh"},{"gloss":"king O","lemma":"מֶ֫לֶךְ","morphology":"ANcbsd/Ta","strongs":"H4430","surface":"מַלְכָּ֖א","transliteration":"mal.Ka'"},{"gloss":"he will deliver","lemma":"שֵׁיזִב","morphology":"AVei3ms","strongs":"H7804","surface":"יְשֵׁיזִֽב׃","transliteration":"ye.shei.Ziv"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"This verse shows trust in God's power. The word for able points to what God can do, not to what the king can do. Romans 8:31 asks who can stand against those God is for, and Luke 1:37 says nothing is impossible with God. Psalm 27:1-2 speaks of trust in the Lord when enemies come near, and Daniel 6:20-22 later tells of God delivering Daniel from lions. The men's phrase 'if it happens' still leaves the outcome open. Their confidence rests on God's ability and character rather than on any guaranteed result."}{"applyIt":"Before starting a task or answering a message, you might pause and ask whether it is being done with care for the people it touches. You could pick one ordinary thing today, such as a reply to a friend, a chore at home, or a piece of work, and ask how love would shape the way you do it. This is not a test to pass. It is an invitation to let care be the thread that runs through small choices.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAGNT (Translators Amalgamated Greek NT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d.1","crossReferences":[{"ref":"JHN.13.34-JHN.13.35","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1PE.4.8","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1TI.1.5","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1JN.4.7-1JN.4.8","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.12.31-1CO.14.1","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"JHN.15.17","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ROM.13.8-ROM.13.10","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"GAL.5.22","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"The small word en sits right before agapē, placing all the doing inside love, so love is pictured as the setting in which every action happens rather than a tool used afterward.","historicalContext":"This passage is the close of Paul's letter to the church in Corinth, a busy port city where the believers were divided and troubled. Just before this line, the letter urges them to watch, stand firm in the faith, and be courageous and strong. Earlier in the passage, the letter asks that Timothy be welcomed and sent on his way in peace, and it mentions Apollos, who was not willing to come at that time. A rule like this one, placed right after those calls to stand firm, works as a unifying summary for a church that had many quarrels, from divisions and lawsuits to disputes about worship and spiritual gifts.","id":"1CO.16.14","lifeInBibleTimes":"Travel in the ancient world was slow and often risky, so people sent on a journey usually depended on friends and hosts for food, lodging, and help on the road. Sending someone on their way with provisions was therefore a real kindness and not a small courtesy. Earlier in the passage, the letter asks the believers to set Timothy forward on his journey in peace, which shows how much practical help mattered. Everyday acts like this are the kind of ordinary doing that this verse has in mind.","meaning":"The verse sets one guiding rule over everything the believers in Corinth do: all of it should be done in love. It comes right after a short run of commands about staying alert, holding to the faith, and acting with courage and strength. Love here is the word agapē, which in the Christian letters points to a steady, self-giving care for others rather than a passing feeling. The verse does not list particular deeds. It says the whole range of a person's activity, from big choices to small daily matters, should be shaped by that care. The lines that follow show what this looks like in practice, such as honoring people who work hard and serve others.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"greek","textBasis":"critical","words":[{"gloss":"All things","lemma":"πᾶς","morphology":"A-NPN","strongs":"G3956","surface":"πάντα","transliteration":"panta"},{"gloss":"of you","lemma":"σύ","morphology":"P-2GP","strongs":"G4771","surface":"ὑμῶν","transliteration":"humōn"},{"gloss":"in","lemma":"ἐν","morphology":"PREP","strongs":"G1722","surface":"ἐν","transliteration":"en"},{"gloss":"love","lemma":"ἀγάπη","morphology":"N-DSF","strongs":"G0026","surface":"ἀγάπῃ","transliteration":"agapē"},{"gloss":"should be done.","lemma":"γίνομαι","morphology":"V-PNM-3S","strongs":"G1096","surface":"γινέσθω.¶","transliteration":"ginesthō"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"The verse shows that love is meant to shape the whole of life, not only moments of formal worship. Jesus gave a new command to love one another and said this would mark his followers (John 13:34-35). John later writes that love comes from God and that God is love (1 John 4:7-8). Paul connects love with fulfilling the law in Romans 13:8-10, and lists love among the fruit of the Spirit in Galatians 5:22. Taken together, these passages suggest that love is less a single act than the pattern by which faith is lived out day to day."}{"applyIt":"Take a few quiet minutes to think of a time when you saw God keep a promise, or to notice something in your life that has stayed steady when other things did not. You might write it down or say it in a prayer of thanks. If it helps, consider one small way this week to show love for God through a choice you make, such as keeping a commitment you have already made.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAHOT (Translators Amalgamated Hebrew OT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d","crossReferences":[{"ref":"HEB.10.23","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2TH.3.3","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2TI.2.13","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"DAN.9.4","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.1.9","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"NEH.1.5","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1TH.5.24","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ROM.8.28","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"Look closely at hane'eman, translated the faithful here. It carries the article ha- at its front, so the word is definite, pointing to a specific, known God rather than to faithfulness in general.","historicalContext":"The surrounding verses describe how the LORD chose Israel, not because they were numerous, but out of love and because he kept the oath he had sworn to their ancestors, then brought them out of Egypt with a mighty hand. This verse draws the conclusion that since he has acted so faithfully, Israel should know him to be faithful. Other lines in the passage speak of God repaying those who hate him, setting his firm dealing with enemies beside his covenant love for his own. The book's speeches are set just before Israel entered the land of Canaan.","id":"DEU.7.9","lifeInBibleTimes":"A covenant in the ancient world was a formal agreement, often between a king and his people or between two parties, with promises on each side. Such agreements were often sealed with an oath, and the surrounding verses mention an oath God swore to the ancestors. Covenant relationships often came with an expectation of loyalty, which fits the way this verse ties God's faithfulness to the people's love and obedience.","meaning":"This verse tells Israel to know, or come to recognize, that the LORD their God is God and that he is faithful. The word for faithful here means reliable and trustworthy. Because he is faithful, he keeps his covenant, the binding agreement he made with his people, and he shows loving kindness, which the original calls covenant loyalty, to those who love him and keep his commandments. The promise is said to reach a thousand generations, a very long span of time.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"hebrew","textBasis":"leningrad","words":[{"gloss":"and you will know","lemma":"יָדַע","morphology":"Hc/Vqq2ms","strongs":"H3045","surface":"וְיָ֣דַעְתָּ֔","transliteration":"ve.Ya.da'.ta"},{"gloss":"that","lemma":"כִּי","morphology":"HTc","strongs":"H3588A","surface":"כִּֽי־","transliteration":"ki-"},{"gloss":"Yahweh","lemma":"יהוה","morphology":"HNpt","strongs":"H3068G","surface":"יְהוָ֥ה","transliteration":"Yah.weh"},{"gloss":"God your","lemma":"אֱלֹהִים","morphology":"HNcmpc/Sp2ms","strongs":"H0430G","surface":"אֱלֹהֶ֖יךָ","transliteration":"'e.lo.Hei.kha"},{"gloss":"he","lemma":"הוּא","morphology":"HPp3ms","strongs":"H1931","surface":"ה֣וּא","transliteration":"hu'"},{"gloss":"[is] <the> God","lemma":"אֱלֹהִים","morphology":"HTd/Ncmpa","strongs":"H0430G","surface":"הָֽאֱלֹהִ֑ים","transliteration":"ha.'E.lo.Him"},{"gloss":"the God","lemma":"אֵל","morphology":"HTd/Ncmsa","strongs":"H0410G","surface":"הָאֵל֙","transliteration":"ha.'El"},{"gloss":"<the> faithful","lemma":"אָמַן","morphology":"HTd/VNrmsa","strongs":"H0539","surface":"הַֽנֶּאֱמָ֔ן","transliteration":"ha.ne.'e.Man"},{"gloss":"[who] keeps","lemma":"שָׁמַר","morphology":"HVqrmsa","strongs":"H8104G","surface":"שֹׁמֵ֧ר","transliteration":"sho.Mer"},{"gloss":"the covenant","lemma":"בְּרִית","morphology":"HTd/Ncfsa","strongs":"H1285","surface":"הַבְּרִ֣ית","transliteration":"ha.be.Rit"},{"gloss":"and <the> covenant loyalty","lemma":"חֶ֫סֶד","morphology":"HC/Td/Ncmsa","strongs":"H2617A","surface":"וְהַחֶ֗סֶד","transliteration":"ve.ha.Che.sed"},{"gloss":"to [those who] love him","lemma":"אָהֵב","morphology":"HR/Vqrmpc/Sp3ms","strongs":"H0157G","surface":"לְאֹהֲבָ֛יו","transliteration":"le.'o.ha.Vav"},{"gloss":"and to [those who] keep","lemma":"שָׁמַר","morphology":"HC/R/Vqrmpc","strongs":"H8104G","surface":"וּלְשֹׁמְרֵ֥י","transliteration":"u.le.sho.me.Rei"},{"gloss":"commandments his","lemma":"מִצְוָה","morphology":"HNcfpc/Sp3ms","strongs":"H4687","surface":"מִצְוֹתָ֖יו","transliteration":"mitz.vo.tov"},{"gloss":"to a thousand","lemma":"אֶ֫לֶף","morphology":"HR/Ncbsc","strongs":"H0505G","surface":"לְאֶ֥לֶף","transliteration":"le.'E.lef"},{"gloss":"generation[s]","lemma":"דּוֹר","morphology":"HNcmsa","strongs":"H1755","surface":"דּֽוֹר׃","transliteration":"Dor"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"This verse presents God as faithful, meaning he keeps his word and his covenant across many generations. It ties that faithfulness to love and obedience, so the people's response is to love him and keep his commandments. The surrounding lines speak of his love for Israel and of the oath he swore to their ancestors, which shows that his promises rest on his own character. Related passages such as Hebrews 10:23, 1 Corinthians 1:9 and 2 Timothy 2:13 also describe God as faithful, so the idea runs through the wider Bible."}{"applyIt":"Take a moment today to look at the task in front of you and give it your honest effort, even a small one. You might also notice the time you have and ask whether there is a kind or useful thing you could do with it now. There is no pressure here, only an invitation to care about the work your hands find.","citations":[{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"STEP Bible, TAHOT (Translators Amalgamated Hebrew OT), Tyndale House Cambridge","url":"https://stepbible.github.io/STEPBible-Data/"},{"licence":"CC BY 4.0","source":"OpenBible.info cross references","url":"https://www.openbible.info/labs/cross-references/"}],"contentVersion":"518425765b0d","crossReferences":[{"ref":"COL.3.23","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"EPH.5.16","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ROM.12.11","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"PSA.6.5","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"ECC.11.6","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"2CH.31.20-2CH.31.21","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"1CO.9.24","source":"OpenBible"},{"ref":"JHN.9.4","source":"OpenBible"}],"didYouKnow":"Look closely at the word 'asher, which appears twice in this verse. The first use follows the word for all and means whatever, while the second introduces where you are going, pointing to the place of the dead.","historicalContext":"Nearby lines urge the reader to eat bread with joy, drink wine with a merry heart, and enjoy the wife you love all the days of life. This line calls for wholehearted work in that same setting. Other lines note that the race is not always to the swift and that time and chance happen to them all. The book is traditionally linked to Solomon, though its authorship and date are disputed.","id":"ECC.9.10","lifeInBibleTimes":"In the Old Testament, Sheol is the shared place of the dead, usually pictured as a shadowy realm where people are no longer part of ordinary life. Many readers see it as a quiet, silent place rather than a busy one. The Old Testament does not describe it as a place of work, planning, or learning. This helps explain why the verse links the chance to act with life under the sun, which the book keeps returning to.","meaning":"The book urges readers to give full effort to whatever work is in front of them. The reason offered is that where you are going, in Sheol, the place of the dead, there is no work, plan, knowledge or wisdom. The verse does not say that effort is pointless now. It points out that the chance to work, plan and learn belongs to this life, so it is worth doing wholeheartedly while it lasts.","model":"claude-haiku-5-5","originalLanguage":{"language":"hebrew","textBasis":"leningrad","words":[{"gloss":"all","lemma":"כֹּל","morphology":"HNcmsa","strongs":"H3605","surface":"כֹּ֠ל","transliteration":"kol"},{"gloss":"that","lemma":"אֲשֶׁר","morphology":"HTr","strongs":"H0834A","surface":"אֲשֶׁ֨ר","transliteration":"'a.Sher"},{"gloss":"it finds","lemma":"מָצָא","morphology":"HVqi3fs","strongs":"H4672","surface":"תִּמְצָ֧א","transliteration":"tim.Tza'"},{"gloss":"hand your","lemma":"יָד","morphology":"HNcbsc/Sp2ms","strongs":"H3027G","surface":"יָֽדְךָ֛","transliteration":"ya.de.Kha"},{"gloss":"to do","lemma":"עָשָׂה","morphology":"HR/Vqcc","strongs":"H6213A","surface":"לַעֲשׂ֥וֹת","transliteration":"la.'a.Sot"},{"gloss":"with strength your","lemma":"כֹּחַ","morphology":"HR/Ncmsc/Sp2ms","strongs":"H3581B","surface":"בְּכֹחֲךָ֖","transliteration":"be.kho.cha.Kha"},{"gloss":"do","lemma":"עָשָׂה","morphology":"HVqv2ms","strongs":"H6213A","surface":"עֲשֵׂ֑ה","transliteration":"'a.Seh"},{"gloss":"for","lemma":"כִּי","morphology":"HTc","strongs":"H3588A","surface":"כִּי֩","transliteration":"ki"},{"gloss":"there not","lemma":"אַ֫יִן","morphology":"HNcmsc","strongs":"H0369","surface":"אֵ֨ין","transliteration":"'ein"},{"gloss":"[is] work","lemma":"מַעֲשֶׂה","morphology":"HNcmsa","strongs":"H4639G","surface":"מַעֲשֶׂ֤ה","transliteration":"ma.'a.Seh"},{"gloss":"and explanation","lemma":"חֶשְׁבּוֹן","morphology":"HC/Ncmsa","strongs":"H2808","surface":"וְחֶשְׁבּוֹן֙","transliteration":"ve.chesh.bOn"},{"gloss":"and knowledge","lemma":"דַּ֫עַת","morphology":"HC/Ncfsa","strongs":"H1847","surface":"וְדַ֣עַת","transliteration":"ve.Da.'at"},{"gloss":"and wisdom","lemma":"חׇכְמָה","morphology":"HC/Ncfsa","strongs":"H2451","surface":"וְחָכְמָ֔ה","transliteration":"ve.cha.khe.Mah"},{"gloss":"in Sheol","lemma":"שְׁאוֹל","morphology":"HR/Ncbsa","strongs":"H7585","surface":"בִּשְׁא֕וֹל","transliteration":"bish.'ol"},{"gloss":"where","lemma":"אֲשֶׁר","morphology":"HTr","strongs":"H0834A","surface":"אֲשֶׁ֥ר","transliteration":"'a.Sher"},{"gloss":"you","lemma":"אַתָּ֫ה","morphology":"HPp2ms","strongs":"H0859A","surface":"אַתָּ֖ה","transliteration":"'a.Tah"},{"gloss":"[are] going","lemma":"הָלַךְ","morphology":"HVqrmsa","strongs":"H1980G","surface":"הֹלֵ֥ךְ","transliteration":"ho.Lekh"},{"gloss":"there towards","lemma":"שָׁם","morphology":"HD/Sd","strongs":"H8033G","surface":"שָֽׁמָּה׃ ס","transliteration":"Sha.mah"}]},"reviewed":false,"theology":"The verse shows that work, planning and learning belong to this life, so they are worth doing with real effort. It treats life as a gift in which a person can act. Colossians 3:23 links everyday work to serving the Lord, and Ephesians 5:16 urges making the most of the time. John 9:4 speaks of a time when no one can work, which fits the same urgency. Together these passages suggest that faithful work is a good use of the days we are given."}